      "content": "The psychometric claim of a universal \"g\" factor is scientifically questionable because early studies lacked diversity, and the strong link between test scores and wealth indicates cultural bias rather than pure cognitive ability. While psychometricians insist these are neutral tools, the discipline itself has largely retreated from these findings due to ongoing criticism from its own prominent founders regarding historical exclusion and validity. Consequently, the sociological critique is often viewed as more intellectually honest today because it addresses the hidden mechanisms of inequality that the \"neutral\" metric fails to expose. Ultimately, neither side offers a perfect solution, but acknowledging the social construction of these tests is the more rigorous path for modern sociological research.",
